f id="af1">School of Molecular Sciences, University of Warwick, Coventry CV4 7AL, U.K.</aff><author-notes><fn id="au1"><label>*</label><p>Present address: School of Pharmacy and Biology, Sunderland Polytechnic, Sunderland, U.K.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>01</month><year>1971</year></pub-date><volume>121</volume><issue>1</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="pmc-issue-id">120303</issue-id><fpage>117</fpage><lpage>124</lpage><pub-history><event event-type="pmc-release"><date><day>01</day><month>01</month><year>1971</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-live"><date><day>07</day><month>09</month><year>2005</year></date></event><event event-type="pmc-last-change"><date iso-8601-date="2019-05-01 18:56:14.597"><day>01</day><month>05</month><year>2019</year></date></event></pub-history><self-uri xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" content-type="pmc-pdf" xlink:href="biochemj00663-0121.pdf"/><abstract><p>1. A method for the preparation of small particles from <italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli</italic> is described. 2. These particles catalyse the ATP-driven reduction of NAD<sup>+</sup> by succinate. 3. ATP utilized/NADH produced ratios below 2.0 were measured and a stoicheiometry of 1 molecule of ATP utilized per molecule of NADH produced is proposed. 4. The reaction is not inhibited by 2,4-dinitrophenol or by oligomycin but is inhibited by other uncouplers such as pentabromophenol and dicoumarol. 5. The specificity of the energy source, the specificity of the electron acceptor, the effects of pH, Mg<sup>2+</sup>, P<sub>i</sub> and temperature have been studied.</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-qastatus</meta-name><meta-value>0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-live</meta-name><meta-value>yes</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-embargo</meta-name><meta-value>no</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>pmc-status-released</meta-name><meta-value>